{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Positivity","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.Basic"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_dilate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_dilate","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L94-L110","doc":"**The q-Borel transform intertwines the two dilations.**  Dilating the\nargument by `q` multiplies the `n`-th coefficient by `qⁿ`; the transform\ndivides by a kernel that does not depend on the coefficients, so the\ntransformed sequence is dilated in exactly the same way:\n\n`B(qⁿ·aₙ) = qⁿ · B(aₙ)`.\n\nThis is the reason the dilation on `x` induces a dilation on the **Borel**\nvariable, hence why a Borel-plane singularity at `ω` moves to `qω` — the\n`shift` field of `QAlienBridge.TwistedQBridgeSystem`, and in turn why the\nindex set of the q-bridge has to be the spiral quotient `E_q = ℂ*/q^ℤ` rather\nthan `ℂ*`.  Recorded here as a lemma rather than left as prose in that file:\nit is the one step of that argument that is a computation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_compose","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_compose","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L85-L92","doc":"Composing two q-Borel divisions adds their exponents — the q-analogue of\n`borelCoeff_compose` (there, applying the single-Borel divisor twice gives the\ndouble-Borel divisor; here the exponents add rather than the factorials squaring).\nThis is the formal-series face of q-acceleration transitivity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff_add","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L79-L83","doc":"The q-Borel coefficient is additive, like its classical counterpart. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.abs_qBorel_le_of_qGevrey","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.abs_qBorel_le_of_qGevrey","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L72-L77","doc":"**q-Gevrey taming at the `n²` order** — the case matching the proved\n`deg_q(cₙ) = n²` growth of the C1 `∞`-side coefficients. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.abs_qBorelCoeff_le_of_growth","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.abs_qBorelCoeff_le_of_growth","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L58-L70","doc":"**q-Gevrey taming, general exponent.** If the coefficients are dominated by\n`C·Aⁿ·q^{e n}`, then dividing by `q^{e n}` leaves them geometrically bounded.\n\nThis is the q-native counterpart of `abs_doubleBorelCoeff_le_of_gevrey2`: there the\ndivisor is `(n!)²`, here it is a power of `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.QGevreyTaming.qBorelCoeff","kind":"def","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/QGevreyTaming.lean#L51-L56","doc":"The q-Borel coefficient for a general exponent sequence `e`: `aₙ / q^{e n}`.\n\nTaking `e n = n²` gives the divisor matching `deg_q(cₙ) = n²`; taking\n`e = qBorelExp d` gives the RSZ level-`d` kernel. ","signatureChunk":0}]}